\section*{Introduction}
A physical collision record carries more information than displacement
alone. Here the observations are two integer lattice coordinates, the
total physical time at unit speed, and the integer occupation of a
specified collision section. Conditioning on the original initial
and terminal section tests identifies the actual event. The aim is
its raw pointwise local limit, with the microscopic physical roof
windows and all integer labels retained, and the resulting
four-coordinate local inverse theorem.

The fixed family consists of equal circular scatterers on the
triangular lattice, with $R\in[9/20,47/100]$. The collision probability
is $d\alpha\,dp/(4\pi)$ and its original section normalization is
$c_*=91/(10000\pi)$. The event \eqref{eq:v76-event}, arithmetic
reference \eqref{eq:v76-reference}, and target \eqref{eq:v76-target}
are unchanged. Occupation counts the initial state and excludes
the terminal state, whose membership is tested separately.
The unit-speed flight bounds
$\tau_-=3/50$ and $\tau_+\le\sqrt{3847/124}<28/5$, the all-center
budgets and the strict marked-foot standoff $\sqrt{1827}/200$
are inherited proved physical geometry. Every reflection in the
argument is an actual first collision.

